        beleg: "Richy Müller, who races cars, has stayed in the same Tatort role since 2008 and campaigns as ambassador of a children’s intensive-care facility; Viggo Mortensen, who lived in four countries as a child, is at home in three languages and had himself taught cave diving for ‘Thirteen Lives’; Foster and his nameless octopus, Goodall and the chimpanzee David Greybeard, Salceda and the sloth Velcro; Velázquez in 'Las Meninas,' where 'the boundary between viewer and viewed blurs'; Beltracchi, whose art forgeries are described as 'merging with the spirit of dead masters'; Carl Rogers' unconditionally accepting therapeutic presence for the person before him; Mario Barth, who since his stage debut in 2001 'merged from then on with a stage persona that spoke to a mass audience' – the Berlin working-class guy character; François Damiens, who staged more than 400 hidden-camera pranks between 1999 and 2005 under a deliberately unlikeable alter ego and to this day shields his own private life so completely that almost nothing is known about his partner or his sons – merging with the role as protection from his own visibility; Keanu Reeves' merging with individual iconic roles (Neo, John Wick), without ever living out the star facade that comes with them; Elizabeth Barrett Browning, whose twenty years of isolation in her father's house dissolved, within twenty months of correspondence, into the one central bond of the rest of her life – 'the SX9 invests her entire energy in the one relationship that counts, letting everything else – even her own safety – fall away.'; Kris Marshall, who played the same character in 'My Family' for eleven years and embodied the same advertising character for over a decade, before leaving 'Death in Paradise,' by his own account, 'to spend more time with his family' – few bonds, but very long-held ones instead of many short ones; Mother Mary (Bible portrait), whose entire life centered on the one bond with her son, beneath the cross, at the empty tomb, and beyond: 'I am here, because you are' – no role, no network, but the one bond that was never let go; Ed Sheeran, who deliberately keeps his marriage to childhood friend Cherry Seaborn out of the public eye and even paused touring entirely in 2019 to protect that one relationship; Jon Bon Jovi, who never left his high-school sweetheart Dorothea Hurley after their elopement in Las Vegas in 1989 and stayed with the same band since 1983 – the same capacity for merging applies equally to his marriage and his band; David Guetta, who almost never sings on his own global hits, merging instead with a rotating guest voice, and whose Ibiza party series 'F*** Me I'm Famous!' has run under the same name since 2002 despite several venue changes – the one institution matters more than any single night; Jürgen Drews, married to Ramona since 1994 and loyal for decades to the same Mallorca audience that awaits him summer after summer – the same wife, the same island, the same celebration, reaffirmed again and again rather than won once and forgotten.; Peter Maffay, whose dragon character Tabaluga, created in 1983, grew over forty years from concept album to musical and animated series to his own foundation – not a side project alongside his music career, but increasingly its actual center.; Ringo Starr, who has been married to Barbara Bach since 1981 and went through rehab together with her in 1988 rather than splitting up under the crisis – the same capacity for merging applies to the marriage as much as to the band: when tensions escalated during the recording of the 'White Album' in 1968, he briefly left the Beatles and returned as soon as the others explicitly asked him back.; Willie Nelson, whose musical partnership with his sister Bobbie outlasted all four of his marriages and every upheaval in his career, from singing gospel together as children to her death in 2022 – a single, lifelong bond that stayed more central than any of his more turbulent romantic relationships.; Owen Wilson, whose more than thirty-year writing partnership with Wes Anderson remained the one reliable constant of his life, while one of his daughters, according to her mother, has never been met; Mads Mikkelsen, together with choreographer Hanne Jacobsen since 1987, a bond that never became a public topic – the same capacity for complete merging that carries his minimalist acting style applies privately to the one, decades-long relationship; Amber Benson, whose writing and audio-drama partnership with Christopher Golden has lasted since 2001 across changing formats – from comics through the 'Ghosts of Albion' novels to the Audible drama 'Slayers: A Buffyverse Story' (2023) – the merging here is directed at the shared work, across more than two decades.; Stefan Homburg, who has held for decades to the same economic-policy conviction since his first public criticism of the euro in 1997, increasingly identifying with it completely – no change of topic, no spreading across shifting debates, but the one position that carries his entire public work.; Dustin Hoffman, who spent weeks with autistic people for 'Rain Man' and stayed awake and starved himself for days for 'Marathon Man,' to actually feel his character's exhaustion rather than merely act it – not an approach to a role, but complete, months-long merging with it, until almost nothing of his own person seems left."
